      <p>A kitchen or bathroom remodel is one of the bigger decisions a homeowner makes. They look at ideas for weeks, set a budget, collect several quotes and often wait for the right season. Google Ads can reach them at every step, but only if the account is built for a long decision and judged on what it actually signs.</p>

      <h2>Separate the planners from the ready-to-hire</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ready to hire:</strong> "kitchen remodeling contractor near me", "bathroom remodel company". The highest intent and the core of the budget</li>
        <li><strong>Planning and cost:</strong> "bathroom remodel cost", "kitchen renovation ideas". Earlier in the decision, worth reaching at lower bids with a consultation offer</li>
        <li><strong>Specific projects:</strong> walk-in showers, tub-to-shower conversions, cabinet refacing. Clear intent and often a distinct price point</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each group gets its own ads and landing page. Planning searches should never share a budget with ready-to-hire searches, or they will quietly absorb it.</p>

      <h2>Filter lead quality before it reaches you</h2>
      <p>The biggest frustration in remodeling accounts is not too few leads, it is the wrong ones: small repair jobs, DIY browsers and people collecting the cheapest possible quote. The campaign can filter much of this out.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ads and pages that state typical project ranges or a minimum job size</li>
        <li>Clear service areas, so distant projects you would decline do not enquire</li>
        <li>A short form that asks about project type, timing and budget range</li>
        <li>Negatives for DIY, materials and small repairs</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Measure signed contracts</h2>
      <p>A remodeling lead can take weeks or months to become a contract. If the account only counts form fills, it will optimise for whoever fills in forms most cheaply, which is rarely the homeowner about to spend a large sum.</p>
      <p>Track the steps that matter: consultation booked, quote sent, contract signed, with the project value. Feeding signed projects back to the campaigns that produced them shows the real cost per project and lets bidding favour the searches that sign. <a href="/learn/how-long-does-google-ads-take-to-work/">How long does it take for Google Ads to work?</a> explains why long sales cycles need patience in the numbers.</p>

      <h2>Show the work</h2>
      <p>Remodeling is a visual purchase. Image assets showing your own finished projects, landing pages with real photographs and clear before-and-after examples build the confidence a large purchase needs. Use only images of work you have actually done.</p>
      <p>Once Search is proven profitable, visual campaigns such as Demand Gen or YouTube can reach homeowners while they browse for ideas. They build demand rather than capture it, so judge them over a longer period.</p>

      <h2>Where remodeling budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>DIY searches: how to tile a bathroom, how to install cabinets</li>
        <li>Materials and fixtures: cabinets for sale, vanity units, flooring prices</li>
        <li>Small repairs you do not take on, such as a single tap or tile</li>
        <li>Design-only and inspiration searches that never become projects</li>
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/learn/how-to-find-negative-keywords/">How to find negative keywords</a> covers how to build and maintain the list.</p>

